T*
(Background: Whether psychotherapy-induced clinical improvement is accompanied by changes) Tj
T*
(in neurobiological biomarkers is unclear. Methods: 82 adults with generalized anxiety) Tj
T*
(disorder \(mean age 41 years\) were randomized to acceptance-based cognitive-behavioral) Tj
T*
(group therapy \(n=41\) or supportive counseling \(n=41\) delivered over 14 weeks. Plasma) Tj
T*
(neurotrophic factor levels and anxiety severity \(Hamilton Anxiety Scale\) were measured at) Tj
T*
(baseline and post-treatment. Results: Both treatment groups showed significant increases) Tj
T*
(in plasma neurotrophic factor from baseline to post-treatment \(supportive therapy: mean) Tj
T*
(increase 2031.4 pg/mL, t=4.79, p<0.001; acceptance-based therapy: mean increase 1963.2) Tj
T*
(pg/mL, t=4.42, p<0.001\). Anxiety symptoms decreased in both groups. However, changes in) Tj
T*
(plasma neurotrophic factor did not correlate with reductions in anxiety symptom severity) Tj
T*
(in either treatment modality. Conclusions: Both active psychological treatments were) Tj
T*
(associated with increases in circulating neurotrophic factor levels; however, the absence) Tj
T*
(of correlation with symptom improvement suggests neurotrophic factor elevation may) Tj
T*
(represent a general response to treatment engagement rather than a specific mechanistic) Tj
T*
(pathway. These findings should be interpreted cautiously and do not establish a direct) Tj
T*
(link between biomarker changes and clinical outcomes.) Tj
